  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and warmer today.\n\nHigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic today and a southerly flow \nwill allow for temperatures to be about 5 to 7 degrees above \naverage this afternoon. Although we can't rule out a shower in \nspots between the Appalachian Front and the Shenandoah Valley \nthis afternoon, most areas will remain dry. High temperatures \nmay approach 90.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and a few thunderstorms late tonight \nthrough Thursday night.\n\nAs the high breaks down and continues to slide to the east\ntonight into early Thursday, a cold front will approach the \nregion from the northwest. It will be relatively slow in \nmovement to the south and east. A couple of showers or a rumble\nof thunder could develop along the front and move into western\nMaryland overnight tonight shortly after midnight and continue\nto propagate southeastward throughout the night. Due to the lack\nof daytime heating, the convection should be mainly due to low-\nlevel moisture and gradual lift from an approaching cold front.\nSome upper-level energy and a jet streak aloft could add \nadditional lift to showers and a couple of thunderstorms across \nthe region during the day Thursday and into Thursday night. \nHowever, a westerly component to the wind in the lower levels \ncould act as a downsloping effect and squash some of this \nconvection in a few places. Highs Thursday could reach the upper\n80s to near 90 and dewpoint temperatures could reach the upper \n60s to low 70s. The front should be slow to move to the south \nand east that showers and thunderstorms could linger well into \nthe night Thursday night. Showers and any thunderstorms should \nbecome more spotty or isolated Friday morning with the front \nnearly stalling across the Virginia Piedmont. Highs Friday \nshould be a couple of degrees cooler due to cloud cover, \nconvection and a northerly component to the wind on the north \nside of the front.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances continue\nthis weekend and into early next week.\n\nOn Saturday, surface high pressure over New England shifts offshore \nas a cold front approaches from the northwest. Surface winds shift \nto southerly, ushering in a more humid airmass. With increased \nhumidity, showers and thunderstorms are possible Saturday and Sunday \nalthough are expected to remain below severe criteria. \n\nGoing into next week, precipitation chances linger but remain low \n(less than 20%). A low pressure system is currently forecast to \ntrack across the Great Lakes with the associated cold fronts \nimpacting the Mid-Atlantic. Temperatures will be in the 70s and\n80s each day with high humidity values yielding heat indices in\nthe low 90s for some.",
